/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - On the test bed only the shaft carries work out of the engine, so two quantities are measured there: torque T, the turning moment in newton metres (N·m), and speed N, the rotation rate in revolutions per second (rev/s).
   - BP is brake power in watts (W); N is shaft speed in rev/s; T is torque in N·m.
   - Brake refers to the brake that loads the engine, so BP is the power handed to the outside world.
   - Torque alone cannot compare two engines of different size.
   - bmep is brake mean effective pressure in pascals (N/m²); Vd is the swept volume in m³; N is speed in rev/s; nR = 2 for a four-stroke (one cycle in two revolutions) and nR = 1 for a two-stroke.
   - Put imep in place of bmep, and indicated torque in place of T, to get indicated mean effective pressure.
   - Fuel power is the rate at which chemical energy enters the engine: fuel power = mf × CV, where mf is fuel mass flow in kg/s and CV is the calorific value in J/kg (lower calorific value about 44 MJ/kg for petrol and 42.5 MJ/kg for diesel).
   - ηbth = BP / (mf × CV) and ηith = IP / (mf × CV)
   - ηbth is brake thermal efficiency, ηith is indicated thermal efficiency, both pure fractions (multiply by 100 for per cent).
   - Volumetric efficiency measures breathing: ηvol = mass of air actually trapped per cycle ÷ (ρa × Vd), where ρa is the density of air outside the engine in kg/m³.
*/
