/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Power leaves an engine down the shaft.
   - Indicated power IP is the rate of work done by the gas on the piston.
   - FP = IP − BP ηmech = BP / IP
   - IP, BP and FP are in the same unit, usually kW; ηmech is a pure number, often given as a percentage.
   - Brake power is computed from two measured quantities, torque and speed.
   - BP is brake power in W; T is the torque absorbed by the dynamometer in N·m; N is engine speed in rev/min; the factor 2π/60 converts rev/min to rad/s.
   - Torque and speed are read directly, so BP is never in doubt.
   - IP = imep × Vd × N / (60 × nR)
   - imep is indicated mean effective pressure in kPa; Vd is swept volume of one cylinder in m³; N is speed in rev/min; nR is 2 for a four-stroke and 1 for a two-stroke; IP is in kW for one cylinder.
   - nR is the number of revolutions per power stroke.
*/
